===== HOW DRAWING ON A PLANE WORKS =====

Think of it like making a TV screen show a picture you drew:

STEP 1: GET A PIECE OF PAPER (Canvas)
- Your computer creates an invisible piece of paper called a "canvas"
- This paper has a width and height (like 512x512 pixels)
- Each pixel is like a tiny dot you can color
- IN CODE: this.canvas = document.createElement('canvas');

STEP 2: GET A PAINTBRUSH (Context)
- The computer gives you a magic paintbrush called "context"
- This brush can draw circles, rectangles, lines, and text
- You tell the brush what color to use and where to draw
- IN CODE: this.brush = this.canvas.getContext('2d');

STEP 3: DRAW YOUR PICTURE
- You use the brush to draw on the paper
- "Draw a red circle here"
- "Draw a blue rectangle there" 
- "Write some text at the top"
- The computer remembers everything you drew
- IN CODE: This happens in the drawContent() function

STEP 4: TAKE A PHOTO OF YOUR DRAWING (Texture)
- Wonderland Engine takes a "photo" of your drawing
- This photo is called a "texture"
- It's like scanning your paper drawing into a computer file
- IN CODE: this.texture = this.engine.textures.create(this.canvas);

STEP 5: PUT THE PHOTO ON PAINT (Material)
- In Wonderland Engine, objects have "materials" (like paint)
- You stick your texture (photo) onto the material
- It's like putting a sticker on something
- IN CODE: this.material.flatTexture = this.texture;

STEP 6: PAINT THE 3D OBJECT
- The material (with your drawing on it) gets painted onto your 3D object
- If your object is a flat plane, it looks like a TV screen showing your drawing
- If your object is curved, your drawing wraps around it
- IN CODE: This happens automatically when you assign the material

THE MAGIC LOOP (Animation):
- If you want animation, you repeat steps 3-6 over and over
- Each time you draw something slightly different
- Like making a flip book - each page is a little different
- When played fast, it looks like movement
- IN CODE: The update() function does this every frame

REAL WORLD EXAMPLE:
Imagine you're making a digital picture frame:

1. You get a blank piece of paper (canvas)
   CODE: this.canvas = document.createElement('canvas');

2. You get some markers (context/brush)
   CODE: this.brush = this.canvas.getContext('2d');

3. You draw a smiley face (your drawing code)
   CODE: this.drawCircle(100, 100, 50, '#ffff00'); // face
          this.drawCircle(80, 80, 5, '#000000');   // left eye
          this.drawCircle(120, 80, 5, '#000000');  // right eye

4. You take a photo of your drawing (texture)
   CODE: this.texture = this.engine.textures.create(this.canvas);

5. You print that photo on special paper (material)
   CODE: this.material.flatTexture = this.texture;

6. You put that paper in a picture frame (your 3D plane object)
   CODE: This happens automatically in Wonderland Engine

If you want the smiley face to blink:
- Every few seconds, you draw a new picture (eyes closed, then open)
- Take a new photo each time
- Replace the photo in the frame
- It looks like the smiley face is blinking!
- IN CODE: In update(), change eye size based on this.time

CODE BREAKDOWN OF THE TEMPLATE:

setupCanvas() - Creates your paper and gets your brush
drawContent() - Where YOU draw your picture
showDrawing() - Takes photo and puts it on the material
update() - Keeps redrawing for animation

DRAWING FUNCTIONS YOU CAN USE:
this.drawCircle(x, y, size, color) - Draws a circle
this.drawRectangle(x, y, width, height, color) - Draws a rectangle
this.drawLine(x1, y1, x2, y2, color, thickness) - Draws a line
this.drawText(text, x, y, font, color) - Writes text

WHY THIS IS COOL:
- You can draw ANYTHING: graphs, games, animations, art
- It updates in real-time in your 3D world
- Other people in VR/AR can see your drawings
- You can make interactive displays, control panels, or art
- All with simple drawing commands!

SIMPLE SUMMARY:
Draw on invisible paper → Take photo → Stick photo on 3D object → Magic!

The computer handles all the hard work. You just tell it what to draw and where using the drawing functions in drawContent().

WHAT YOU NEED TO CHANGE:
